<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · War and march · 1910 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Amhrán na bhFiann</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The national anthem of Ireland: a marching song of 1910 by Peadar Kearney and Patrick Heeney, sung today mostly in Irish.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Peadar Kearney (English words); Liam Ó Rinn (Irish translation)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Patrick Heeney and Peadar Kearney</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Irish nationalists and the Irish Volunteers; the Irish state</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Irish, English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Dublin, Ireland</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>composed late 1909 or early 1910; words published 1912; Irish translation printed 1923; adopted as the national anthem 12 July 1926</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (English lyrics by Peadar Kearney, published 1912 (EU copyright expired 1 January 2013, 70 years after his death); music by Kearney and Patrick Heeney (d. 1911); Irish translation by Liam Ó Rinn (1886–1943), printed 1923)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The song was first written in English as &quot;A Soldier's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>&quot;, &quot;early in 1910 or late in 1909&quot;, with words by Peadar Kearney and music by his childhood friend and neighbour Patrick Heeney, who composed it on his melodeon with Kearney's help on the refrain; Seán Rogan, later of the Irish Citizen Army, may also have helped and first wrote it down in notation. Kearney wrote much of the text in the Swiss Café on O'Connell Street in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>. Rejected by The United Irishman, the text was published in 1912 in Bulmer Hobson's magazine Irish Freedom. The Irish Volunteers took it up as a marching song; rebels sang it in the General Post Office during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-rising">Easter Rising</a> of 1916, and it grew more popular among the prisoners in Frongoch internment camp afterwards. Sheet music followed in Dublin in late 1916, and in December 1916 Victor Herbert published arrangements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, where the first commercial recording was made in early 1917.</p><p>During the War of Independence it was called the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinn-fein">Sinn Féin</a> anthem&quot;, and British forces confiscated copies as seditious; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eamon-de-valera">Éamon de Valera</a>'s platform at the East Clare by-election of June 1917 carried its opening lines on a banner. In the Civil War of 1922–23 both the National Army and the anti-Treaty &quot;Irregulars&quot; sang it. The Irish Free State at first adopted no anthem: ex-unionists still regarded &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot; as theirs, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-t-cosgrave">W. T. Cosgrave</a>'s government feared both deepening unionist hostility in Northern Ireland and leaving the song to its republican opponents. &quot;Let Erin Remember&quot; served abroad, at the 1924 Olympics among other events. On 12 July 1926 the Executive Council quietly adopted &quot;The Soldier's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>&quot; as the national anthem, and from 1928 the Army band played only its chorus, in an official arrangement by Fritz Brase from 1929. The state bought the copyright in 1934 for £1,200 and again in 1965; under EU law the English words passed out of copyright on 1 January 2013.</p><p>The Irish translation by Liam Ó Rinn, later chief translator of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oireachtas">Oireachtas</a>, was printed in the Freeman's Journal on 3 April 1923 and in the Defence Forces' magazine An tÓglach that November; it may date from as early as 1917. Encouraged by the Gaelic Athletic Association from the 1930s, the Irish words came to eclipse the English, though no Irish version has ever been officially adopted. Its opening words share a phrase with the name of the party <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eamon-de-valera">Éamon de Valera</a> founded in 1926, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fianna-fail">Fianna Fáil</a>, a name the Volunteers had used earlier; ministers and a 2018 Seanad report held that the anthem carries no party meaning, since the translation came first.</p><p>Only the chorus is the official anthem, and the President's salute is made of its first and last bars. It is played at all GAA matches, including those in Northern Ireland, where unionists regard it as the anthem of the Republic alone; the Northern Ireland government drafted bans on it in the 1930s, and all-island rugby and hockey teams play Phil Coulter's &quot;Ireland's Call&quot; instead or as well. Critics have called it militaristic, anti-British or musically weak, and proposals to replace it recur; a 2017 poll found 84% in favour of keeping it, and the Seanad's 2018 report recommended no change to the words and an official version in Irish Sign Language.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A rebel marching song in which soldiers pledge to fight for Ireland's freedom from English rule, calling on Irish people of every background to join the struggle and recalling earlier risings and help from Irish Americans. Its mood is martial and rousing.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Irish</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">We'll sing a song, a soldier's song,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">With cheering rousing chorus,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">As round our blazing fires we throng,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">The starry heavens o'er us;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">Impatient for the coming fight,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">And as we wait the morning's light,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">Here in the silence of the night,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">We'll chant a soldier's song.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">Soldiers are we , whose lives are pledged to Ireland;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">Some have come from a land beyond the wave.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">Sworn to be free, No more our ancient sire land</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">Shall shelter the despot or the slave.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">Tonight we man the gap of danger</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">In Erin's cause, come woe or weal</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">'Mid cannons' roar and rifles peal,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">We'll chant a soldier's song.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">In valley green, on towering crag,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">Our fathers fought before us,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">And conquered 'neath the same old flag</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">That's proudly floating o'er us.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">We're children of a fighting race,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">That never yet has known disgrace,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">And as we march, the foe to face,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">We'll chant a soldier's song.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">Sons of the Gael! Men of the Pale!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">The long watched day is breaking;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">The serried ranks of Inisfail</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">Shall set the Tyrant quaking.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">Our camp fires now are burning low;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">See in the east a silv'ry glow,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">Out yonder waits the Saxon foe,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ga">So chant a soldier's song.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Peadar Kearney (1912). Text from Wikisource (<a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/A_Soldier's_Song_(Kearney%2FHeeney)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A Soldier's Song (Kearney/Heeney)</a>); public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
