<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Soldiers of Erin, the Rallying Song of the Irish Volunteers (Victor Herbert)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1916</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">George Potter, first commercial recording</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1917</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Irish version by Liam Ó Rinn</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1923</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Rosc Catha na nÓglach (T. F. O'Rahilly)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">early 1920s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Irish March, no. 1 (Fritz Brase)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1924</span><span class="sg-chain-h">medley ending with its air</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Official arrangement of the chorus (Fritz Brase)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1929</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Extra verse by Peadar Kearney, The Irish Press</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1938</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new verse on partition</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Our Lady's Choral Society with the Radio Éireann Symphony Orchestra</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1965</span><span class="sg-chain-h">first recording in Irish</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The National Anthem (Frank Ormsby)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2017</span><span class="sg-chain-h">poem parodying the Irish text</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Irish Sign Language version</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2018</span><span class="sg-chain-h">translation</span></li></ul></section>
